.TH PBUS 1 "2026-10-25" "pbus @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pbus \- declare, subscribe to and emit system events
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pbus
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B pbus
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pbus
drives the
.BR pbusd (8)
event dispatcher. An event is a name; a listener is a command run when that
event is emitted. Listeners keep no process waiting on anything: the daemon
starts them itself, asynchronously.
.PP
With no argument, on a terminal,
.B pbus
opens an interactive session accepting the same commands.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Events
.TP
.B list-events
List events, how many listeners each has, and whether they require a key.
.TP
.BI "show-event " name
Detail one event.
.TP
.BI "register-event " name " [\-d " description "] [\-\-no\-key] [\-p " ere "] [\-s] [\-f]"
Declare an event and print, once only, the key emitters must present. With
.BR \-\-no\-key ,
the event is open to any local emission. With
.BR \-s ,
the key is kept in the user's keyring. With
.BR \-p / \-\-payload\-pattern ,
the daemon refuses any emission whose payload does not match that extended
regular expression, before a single listener runs;
.B ^$
declares an event that carries no payload. An expression that cannot be
compiled refuses every emission rather than letting them through.
.TP
.BI "unregister-event " name " [\-\-purge\-listeners]"
Remove an event, its secret, and optionally its listeners.
.TP
.BI "rotate-key " name " [\-s]"
Replace the key. The previous one stops being valid at once.
.TP
.BI "save-key " name " [\-k " key ]
Put a key in the keyring. Without
.BR \-k ,
the key is prompted for without echo.
.SS Listeners
.TP
.BI "register-listener " event " \-u " user " [options] " command ...
Register a command to run. The event may be a glob pattern, such as
.BR release.* .
.B \-u
is required: the account the command runs as is never implied, and
.B \-u root
is how root is asked for.
If a single command argument is given it is kept verbatim and interpreted by
the shell, which allows
.BR "cmd1 && cmd2" .
If several arguments are given, each one is quoted.
Options:
.BR \-\-id ,
.BR \-u / \-\-user ,
.BR \-w / \-\-workdir ,
.BR \-t / \-\-timeout ,
.BR \-d / \-\-description ,
.BR \-\-disabled .
.TP
.BI "list-listeners [" event ]
List listeners.
.TP
.BI "show-listener " id
Print a listener's file.
.TP
.BI "unregister-listener " id
Remove a listener.
.TP
.BI "enable " id " | disable " id
Enable or suspend a listener without removing it.
.SS Emission
.TP
.BI "emit " event " [options]"
Emit an event. The payload comes from
.BR \-p / \-\-payload ,
.BR \-F / \-\-payload\-file
or
.BR \-\-stdin .
The key comes from
.BR \-k / \-\-key ,
.BR \-K / \-\-key\-file ,
the
.B PBUS_KEY
variable, or the keyring.
.B emit
waits for the daemon's verdict and exits 0 when the event was accepted, 1 on a
local error, 3 when the key was refused, 4 when the event is unknown, 6 when
the payload does not match the event's
.IR payload_pattern ,
and 5
when no acknowledgement arrived within
.BR \-\-ack\-timeout
(5 seconds by default).
.B \-\-no\-wait
skips the wait entirely. The verdict is sent before the listeners start, so
this never waits for the triggered commands themselves.
.TP
.BI "test " event
Show what would run, without running it.
.SS Misc
.TP
.BR status ", " "log [\-n " N "] [\-f]" ", " reload ", " shell ", " help ", " version
.SH ENVIRONMENT OF A TRIGGERED COMMAND
.TP
.B PBUS_EVENT
Real name of the event emitted.
.TP
.B PBUS_PAYLOAD
Payload, also fed to the command's standard input. It is chosen by the
emitter, so it is untrusted input: quote it
.B and
end the command's options with
.BR \-\- ,
as in
.RB \(lq "cmd \-\- \(dq$PBUS_PAYLOAD\(dq" \(rq .
Quoting alone stops the word splitting and the globbing but not a payload read
as an option, and a single option such as
.B \-\-use\-compress\-program=
is enough to run a program of the emitter's choosing. Where a command takes no
.BR \-\- ,
write
.RB \(lq ./\(dq$PBUS_PAYLOAD\(dq \(rq
for a path;
.B echo
ignores
.B \-\-
and prints it, so prefer
.BR "printf '%s\en'" .
Never pass the payload through a second shell. See
.I payload_pattern
in
.BR pbusd (8)
for refusing a hostile shape outright.
.TP
.B PBUS_PAYLOAD_SIZE
Size of the payload in bytes, always set. Above 100000 bytes the payload is
left out of the environment altogether — the kernel refuses an
.BR execve (2)
whose environment holds a string that long, which would stop the command from
running at all — so a command that must cope with any size should read
standard input and use this to tell an empty payload from an omitted one.
.TP
.B PBUS_EVENT_ID
Unique id of the emission, repeated in the log.
.TP
.B PBUS_TS
Timestamp of the emission.
.TP
.B PBUS_LISTENER
Identifier of the listener.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pbus/pbus.conf
Daemon configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/pbus/events.d/<name>.event
One event's declaration.
.TP
.I /etc/pbus/listeners.d/<id>.listener
One listener's declaration. Any edit applies to the next event.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pbus/auth/<name>.auth
Salt and hash of the key, readable by root only.
.TP
.I ~/.config/pbus/keys/<name>
The user's keyring;
.I /etc/pbus/keys/<name>
for root.
.SH KEY LOOKUP ORDER
.BR \-\-key ,
then
.BR \-\-key\-file ,
then
.BR $PBUS_KEY ,
then the keyring.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pbusd (8)
